 "module": "m53850",
 "chapter": "11",
 "title": "Calculus of Parametric Curves",
 "short": "Calculus of Parametric Curves",
 "lead": "Calculus works on a <span data-concept=\"parametric-curve\">parametric curve</span> without first eliminating its <span data-concept=\"parameter\">parameter</span>. This section finds the slope of its <span data-concept=\"tangent-line\">tangent line</span> and its second derivative from $x(t)$ and $y(t),$ the area under it, its <span data-concept=\"arc-length\">arc length</span>, and the area of the surface it sweeps out when it is revolved around the $x$-axis.",
 "objectives": [
  "Determine derivatives and equations of tangents for parametric curves.",
  "Find the area under a parametric curve.",
  "Use the equation for arc length of a parametric curve.",
  "Apply the formula for surface area to a volume generated by a parametric curve."
 ],
 "summary_html": "<ul><li>The <span data-concept=\"derivative\">derivative</span> of the <span data-concept=\"parametric-curve\">parametrically defined curve</span> $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t)$ can be calculated using the formula $\\kdydx=\\frac{\\kyprime(t)}{\\kxprime(t)}.$ Using the <span data-concept=\"derivative\">derivative</span>, we can find the equation of a <span data-concept=\"tangent-line\">tangent line</span> to a <span data-concept=\"parametric-curve\">parametric curve</span>.</li><li>The area between a <span data-concept=\"parametric-curve\">parametric curve</span> and the <em>x</em>-axis can be determined by using the formula $\\kA=\\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}y(t)\\kxprime(t)\\;dt .$</li><li>The <span data-concept=\"arc-length\">arc length</span> of a <span data-concept=\"parametric-curve\">parametric curve</span> can be calculated by using the formula $\\ks=\\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\kdxdt)}^{2}+{(\\kdydt)}^{2}}dt .$</li><li>The <span data-concept=\"surface-area\">surface area</span> of a volume of revolution revolved around the <em>x</em>-axis is given by $\\kS=2\\pi \\int_a^{b}y(t)\\sqrt{{(\\kxprime(t))}^{2}+{(\\kyprime(t))}^{2}}dt .$ If the curve is revolved around the <em>y</em>-axis, then the formula is $\\kS=2\\pi \\int_a^{b}x(t)\\sqrt{{(\\kxprime(t))}^{2}+{(\\kyprime(t))}^{2}}dt .$</li></ul>",
