   "cite": "fs-id1167794065177",
   "prompt": "<p>Show that the total length of the ellipse $x=4\\;\\sin \\theta ,y=3\\;\\cos \\theta$ is $L=16\\int_0^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-e^{2}{\\sin }^{2}\\theta}\\;d\\theta ,$ where $e=\\frac{c}{a}$ and $c=\\sqrt{a^{2}-b^{2}}.$</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "solution": "With $x=4\\sin \\theta$ and $y=3\\cos \\theta ,$ $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta}=4\\cos \\theta$ and $\\frac{dy}{d\\theta}=-3\\sin \\theta ,$ so $\\sqrt{{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta})}^{2}+{(\\frac{dy}{d\\theta})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{16{\\cos }^{2}\\theta +9{\\sin }^{2}\\theta }=\\sqrt{16-7{\\sin }^{2}\\theta }=4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{7}{16}{\\sin }^{2}\\theta }.$ Here $a=4,$ $b=3,$ $c=\\sqrt{a^{2}-b^{2}}=\\sqrt{7}$ and $e=\\frac{c}{a}=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},$ so $e^{2}=\\frac{7}{16}.$ By Equation 11.5 the quarter of the ellipse traced for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ has length $\\int_0^{\\pi /2}4\\sqrt{1-e^{2}{\\sin }^{2}\\theta }\\;d\\theta ,$ and by symmetry the ellipse is four such quarters, so $L=16\\int_0^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-e^{2}{\\sin }^{2}\\theta }\\;d\\theta .$",
    "generated_by": "ai"
   }
  },
